course: biology
lesson: 364
title: The body's rough side: burps, farts, snot, sneezes, hiccups and poo
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Burps, farts, snot, sneezes, hiccups and poo make everyone giggle, but each one is the body doing a useful job: letting gas out, trapping germs, clearing the nose or getting rid of waste.
explain: Think of a fizzy drink: shake it and the gas wants out. Your stomach is the same, so after a can of cola the gas comes back up as a burp.
explain: Your nose is a sticky dust trap, your gut is a home for trillions of bacteria that make gas, and your poo is mostly water and bacteria, not old food.
explain: Everybody does all of these things, teachers and queens included; the only rule is to do them politely and wash your hands.
fact: A burp is gas from the stomach coming back up, swallowed air plus the carbon dioxide bubbles of fizzy drinks, and babies gulp air while feeding, so grown-ups pat their backs.
fact: Most fart gas is made by bacteria in the large gut breaking down fibre from beans, vegetables and bread, plus some swallowed air.
fact: Most fart gas has no smell at all; only a tiny bit of sulphur gas makes the stink, and people pass wind about 10 to 20 times a day.
fact: The nose makes mucus all the time, and most of it is swallowed without you noticing; it traps dust, pollen and germs before they reach the lungs.
fact: Green snot does not always mean bacteria: the colour comes from a substance in white blood cells fighting germs.
fact: A bogey is dried mucus around trapped dust; picking spreads germs to your fingers and on to others and can make the nose bleed.
fact: A sneeze is a reflex that clears the nose, and about 1 person in 4 also sneezes at bright light; fast-camera tests found the air much slower than the 160 kilometres an hour people say, but droplets can float several metres.
fact: The story that your eyes pop out if you sneeze with them open is not true; closing them is just a reflex.
fact: A hiccup is the diaphragm, the big breathing muscle under the lungs, suddenly jerking while the voice box snaps shut; eating fast, fizzy drinks or a full stomach can trigger it.
fact: An American man called Charles Osborne is reported to have hiccupped for about 68 years.
fact: Poo is about three-quarters water, and much of the rest is bacteria, undigested fibre and old cells from the gut lining; it is brown because of a pigment made from old red blood cells.
fact: Beetroot can turn poo and wee pink for a day, which is harmless.
fact: Doctors use a chart of seven poo types, from hard lumps to watery liquid; type 4, a smooth sausage, is ideal, and hard lumps mean you need more water and fibre.
fact: A tummy rumble is gas and liquid squeezed along the gut by its muscles, louder when the stomach is empty, and washing hands with soap for about 20 seconds removes most germs.
exercise: What is a burp? | Gas from the stomach coming back up | Air leaking up and out of the bottom of the lungs | A small hiccup in the throat | Food going down the wrong way
exercise: Why do grown-ups pat a baby's back after feeding? | To help out the air the baby gulped while feeding | To make the baby fall asleep at once | To push the milk down faster into the baby's tummy so it can feed again | To stop the baby from crying for ever
exercise: What makes most fart gas? | Bacteria in the large gut breaking down fibre | The stomach burning up food like a little fire | Air breathed in through the nose | Gas from the lungs
exercise: Why do most farts not smell? | Most of the gas has no smell, only a tiny bit of sulphur gas stinks | The nose gets so used to all your own smells that it stops noticing them at all | Farts only smell in summer | The bacteria eat the smell
exercise: About how many times a day do people pass wind? | About 10 to 20 times | Only about once a week | About 500 times or more | Never, if they are polite
exercise: What does nose mucus do? | Traps dust, pollen and germs before they reach the lungs | Helps you taste the sweetness of sweets and cakes much better | Keeps the nose warm in winter only | Makes your voice deeper
exercise: Where does most snot go when your nose is not running? | It is swallowed without you noticing | It slowly dries up and gets stored inside the brain | It stays stuck up inside the nose for ever | It slowly leaks out through your ears at night
exercise: What makes snot green? | A substance in white blood cells fighting germs | Eating lots of green vegetables like peas and spinach | Grass pollen stuck in the nose | It always means bacteria
exercise: What is a bogey? | Mucus that has dried around trapped dust and dirt | A tiny piece of the inside of the nose that has fallen off | A tiny insect living in the nose | Old food stuck in the nose
exercise: About how many people sneeze when they walk into bright light? | About 1 in 4 | Everyone in the world | Nobody at all | About 1 in a million
exercise: What happens if you sneeze with your eyes open? | Nothing bad, closing them is only a reflex | Your eyes pop right out of your head | You go blind for a day | Your ears burst
exercise: What causes a hiccup? | The diaphragm jerks and the voice box snaps shut | The heart skips a beat and then beats twice as hard | The stomach turns upside down | Air gets stuck in your ears
exercise: About how long did Charles Osborne reportedly hiccup? | About 68 years | About 68 minutes | About 68 days on end | About 6 hours non-stop
exercise: About how much of poo is water? | About three-quarters | Almost none | Exactly half | All of it
exercise: Why is poo brown? | Because of a pigment made from old red blood cells | Because of all the chocolate and gravy that people eat | Because of mud in the gut | Because all food is brown
exercise: Which poo type on the doctor's chart is the ideal one? | Type 4, a smooth sausage | Type 1, hard little lumps | Type 7, all watery liquid | Type 9, perfectly round balls
exercise: Why is a tummy rumble louder before lunch? | The stomach is empty, so nothing muffles the noise | The stomach is shouting for food on purpose | Rumbles only ever happen when you are hungry, never after a meal | The gut stops working after lunch
exercise: How long should you wash your hands with soap? | About 20 seconds, the time to sing Happy Birthday twice | About 1 second | About 10 minutes | Until the water runs cold
test: «had a can of cola and now I can't stop burping, why lol» What is the reason? | The bubbles of carbon dioxide in the drink come back up | Cola makes the stomach shrink so the air gets squeezed out | Burping is caused by sugar only | Cola is made of air
test: Why do beans make people windier than chips? | Beans have more fibre for the gut bacteria to break down into gas | Beans are full of tiny air bubbles that get trapped inside them when they are cooked | Chips soak up all the gas | Beans are cooked in fizzy water
test: «green snot = I need antibiotics, mum» What is right? | Not always; the green comes from white blood cells fighting any germs | Yes, green snot always means bacteria | Yes, green snot means you are allergic | No, green snot means you ate spinach
test: Why might a cold spread faster in a class full of nose pickers? | Picking puts germs on fingers, and fingers touch everything | Nose picking makes the germs grow bigger and stronger | Picking makes noses run faster | Colds spread through bogeys flying through the air across the classroom
test: Why do people sneeze into their elbow and not their hand? | Hands touch door handles and people, elbows do not | Elbows can catch more air and hold the whole sneeze in | Sneezing into hands hurts the fingers | It makes the sneeze quieter
test: «my mate says sneezes go 160 km an hour, faster than a car» What did fast-camera tests find? | The air is much slower, nearer a fast bicycle, though droplets can float several metres | It is true, sneezes beat racing cars | Sneezes do not move at all | Sneezes go faster than sound
test: In a cartoon, Tom gets hiccups and each hic lifts him a metre off the ground until Jerry scares them away. What is a real hiccup? | A jerk of the diaphragm with the voice box snapping shut, which cannot lift you | A jump made by the legs | A small explosion in the stomach | A cough that only cats get
test: «help!! ate beetroot salad and now my wee's pink, am i dying??» What is true? | Beetroot can turn poo and wee pink for a day, and it is harmless | Yes, call an ambulance at once | Beetroot has turned all your blood pink, and it is leaking into your wee | Only cats have this problem
test: «my hands look clean, why wash after the loo, it's a waste of time» What is right? | Germs are too small to see, so clean-looking hands can carry them | Germs only ever live on hands that look dirty or smell bad | Toilets have no germs | Water alone always kills every germ
test: Why do doctors use a poo chart instead of asking people to describe their poo? | Pictures let everyone point to the same type without awkward words | Doctors cannot understand words | Poo changes shape when you describe it | The chart makes poo healthier, because looking at the right picture helps the gut copy it
test: Count them: a doctor's poo chart has seven types, and types 3 and 4 are the healthy ones. How many types are not? | 5 | 2 | 7 | 4
test: Why do so many hiccup cures exist when few are proven? | Hiccups usually stop on their own, so whatever you tried last seems to work | Every cure works for everybody | Doctors invented them all as jokes | Hiccups are caused by a different germ each time
exam: Identify the principal origin of the gas expelled from the large intestine. | The fermentation of dietary fibre by bacteria | The combustion of food within the stomach | The inhalation of air through the nose | The release of gas from the lungs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the green colouration of nasal mucus? | It derives from a substance within white blood cells and does not invariably indicate bacteria | It invariably indicates a bacterial infection | It derives from the consumption of green vegetables | It indicates that the mucus has been retained for one week
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of human faeces. | They consist of approximately three-quarters water, together with bacteria and fibre | They consist almost wholly of undigested meat | They consist of dried blood | They contain no bacteria whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the duration of handwashing with soap recommended for the removal of most germs. | Approximately twenty seconds | Approximately one second only | Approximately one hour on each occasion | No duration, water alone being sufficient
